      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Chronic total occlusion (CTO) of the left main coronary artery (LMCA) is a rare manifestation of coronary artery disease (CAD) and defined by a total absence of antegrade blood flow to the left anterior descending (LAD) and circumflex (Cx) system. Patients are at high risk of myocardial ischaemia as a sizeable area of myocardium is at risk and thus require urgent intervention. Surgery is the treatment of choice especially with CTO lesions as percutaneous coronary intervention has limited success with a high restenosis rate. We report a rare case of a young Chinese male who presented acutely with a myocardial infarction and discuss the aetiology and peri-operative technical considerations for successful surgery with this condition."},
